## The record was wrong about what the seats reviewed

The final-review seat recomputed seat-envelope timestamps against stage capture
times instead of trusting the manifest, and found that **three of this bead's
four passing attempts named an artifact the seats had never been handed:**

| attempt | recorded | actually reviewed |
|---|---|---|
| `plan.r2` | `1551aa66` | codex read `96c0efd0`; opus read `1551aa66` |
| `implement.r4` | `67a23225` | `e54ff6f8` — codex's envelope predates `67a23225` by 13 minutes |
| `verify.r3` | `4d5924bc` | `3f7aaa40` |
| `docs.r7` | `421218f7` | `421218f7` — clean |

An earlier draft of this artifact called the run "a partial answer" to *which
artifacts did a seat approve?*. That was too kind: **it returned a wrong one**,
on the bead offered as proof of the model.

**And `plan.r2` is worse than a mislabel.** Its two seats did not read the same
bytes: codex was handed the packet frozen at `96c0efd0`, its optionals were then
folded to produce `1551aa66`, and the opus packet froze that. The record shows a
unanimous two-seat panel. **Unanimous quorum over seats that reviewed different
artifacts is not quorum**, and nothing in the record signalled it.

Corrections are appended — `plan.r4`, `implement.r5`, `verify.r4` — each naming
the bytes its seats actually read, re-running nothing and granting no new
approval. The originals stay: deleting them would make the record tidier and less
true.

**The root cause is structural, and it is the best argument for `etude-4ed` this
epic has produced.** The bootstrap path takes reviewed-stage, artifact and seats
as author-supplied arguments, so nothing forces a panel to share a prompt or to
name what it read. `etude gate` cannot make either mistake — it composes one
prompt, runs every seat against it, and resolves the reviewed stage itself. All
of it is now written onto `etude-4ed`.

